> Data modeling:
Data modeling is the process of organizing and structuring data into tables, relationships, and constraints so it can be stored, managed, and queried efficiently. It helps reduce data redundancy, improve data quality, and optimize query performance. In data warehousing, data modeling is commonly implemented using Star Schema or Snowflake Schema, where fact tables store business metrics and dimension tables store descriptive attributes.

Key Points
Organize data into tables and relationships
Improve data quality
Reduce redundancy
Improve query performance
Supports analytics and reporting

>Types of Data Models : 3 types

Conceptual Model
High-level business view
Defines entities and relationships
Example: Customer, Product, Order

Logical Model
Defines attributes, primary keys, and foreign keys
Independent of any specific database
Ex:Customer(Customer_ID, Name)
Order(Order_ID, Customer_ID, Order_Date)

Physical Model
Database implementation
Includes data types, indexes, partitions, constraints, storage details
Ex:Customer(
   Customer_Key INT,
   Name VARCHAR(100),
   Email VARCHAR(100) )

> Why is data modeling important?
Improves query performance
Reduces redundancy
Maintains data integrity
Makes reporting and analytics easier
Supports scalable ETL pipelines


> OLAP v/s OLTP

| OLTP                 | OLAP                   |
| -------------------- | ---------------------- |
| Transactional        | Analytical             |
| Insert/Update/Delete | Read-heavy             |
| Current data         | Historical data        |
| Normalized           | Denormalized           |
| Fast transactions    | Fast complex queries   |
| Thousands of users   | Fewer analytical users |

Q: Why don't we run reports on OLTP?

It impacts transaction performance. Complex analytical queries can slow down inserts and updates. Therefore, data is moved to a Data Warehouse (OLAP) for reporting.

Q: Which databases are OLTP?
Examples:
MySQL
PostgreSQL
Oracle
SQL Server

Q: Which databases are OLAP?
Examples:
Snowflake
Amazon Redshift
Google BigQuery
Azure Synapse

> Fact v/s Dimension table
| Fact Table              | Dimension Table                 |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Stores business metrics | Stores descriptive attributes   |
| Large number of records | Smaller number of records       |
| Contains foreign keys   | Contains primary key            |
| Numeric values          | Text/descriptive values         |
| Used for calculations   | Used for filtering and grouping |

Q: Can a Fact Table exist without Dimension Tables?
Technically yes, but it loses business context. Dimension tables provide meaningful information like customer names, product names, and dates, making reports understandable.

> star schema :
A Star Schema consists of one central Fact Table connected directly to multiple denormalized Dimension Tables.
              Dim_Date
                 |
Dim_Customer -- Fact_Sales -- Dim_Product
                 |
             Dim_Store
Advantages :
Faster query performance
Fewer joins
Simple to understand
Ideal for BI tools
Disadvantages :
More data redundancy
Uses more storage

> Snowflake Schema
A Snowflake Schema is an extension of the Star Schema where dimension tables are normalized into multiple related tables.

               Dim_Date
                  |
Dim_Customer -- Fact_Sales -- Dim_Product
                                 |
                            Dim_Category
                                 |
                             Dim_Brand
Advantages :
Less redundancy
Better data integrity
Saves storage
Easier to maintain master data
Disadvantages :
More joins
Slightly slower queries
More complex design

> Star vs Snowflake
| Star Schema             | Snowflake Schema      |
| ----------------------- | --------------------- |
| Denormalized dimensions | Normalized dimensions |
| Fewer joins             | More joins            |
| Faster queries          | Slightly slower       |
| More storage            | Less storage          |
| Simpler design          | More complex          |

Q: Which one is better?
It depends on the business requirement.
Star Schema
Better for reporting and dashboards.
Preferred in most data warehouses.
Snowflake Schema
Better when reducing redundancy and maintaining dimension data is important.


