> Types of Fact Table :

1. Transaction Fact Table (Most Common)
Definition : Stores every business transaction.
Ex:
| Order_ID | Customer | Product | Sales |
| -------- | -------- | ------- | ----: |
| 1001     | John     | Laptop  |  1000 |
| 1002     | Alice    | Phone   |   800 |

2. Snapshot Fact Table
Stores the state of a business process at regular intervals (daily, weekly, monthly).
Ex:
| Date   | Product | Inventory |
| ------ | ------- | --------: |
| 01-Jan | Laptop  |       500 |
| 02-Jan | Laptop  |       450 |

3. Accumulating Snapshot Fact Table
Tracks a process with multiple milestones. The same row is updated as the process progresses.
Ex:
| Order_ID | Order Date | Ship Date | Delivery Date |
| -------- | ---------- | --------- | ------------- |
| 1001     | 1-Jan      | 3-Jan     | 5-Jan         |

4. Factless Fact Table
Contains no numeric measures. It records that an event happened.
Ex:
| Student_ID | Course_ID | Date   |
| ---------- | --------- | ------ |
| 101        | CS101     | 01-Jan |

Difference :

| Fact Type             | Stores                  | Example         |
| --------------------- | ----------------------- | --------------- |
| Transaction           | Every transaction       | Sales           |
| Snapshot              | Periodic state          | Daily Inventory |
| Accumulating Snapshot | Process milestones      | Order Tracking  |
| Factless              | Events without measures | Attendance      |


> Types of Dimension Tables

1. Conformed Dimension
Shared across multiple fact tables.
Ex:
Fact_Sales
      |
Dim_Customer
      |
Fact_Returns


2. Role-Playing Dimension
Same dimension used multiple times.
Ex:
Fact_Order

Order_Date

Ship_Date

Delivery_Date


3. Junk Dimension
Stores low-cardinality flags.
Ex:
| Active | Coupon | Online_Order |
| ------ | ------ | ------------ |
| Y      | N      | Y            |

4. Degenerate Dimension
No separate dimension table. Stored directly in the fact table.

5. Mini Dimension
Stores frequently changing attributes.
Examples:
Customer Status
Loyalty Level
Risk Category

6. Slowly Changing Dimension (SCD)
7. Snowflaked Dimension : A normalized dimension.
| Type   | History                |
| ------ | ---------------------- |
| Type 0 | No changes             |
| Type 1 | No history             |
| Type 2 | Full history           |
| Type 3 | Previous value only    |
| Type 4 | Separate history table |
| Type 6 | Hybrid                 |


Conparision :

| Dimension Type | Purpose                         |
| -------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Conformed      | Shared across facts             |
| Role Playing   | Same dimension, different roles |
| Junk           | Flags and indicators            |
| Degenerate     | Stored in fact table            |
| Mini           | Frequently changing attributes  |
| SCD            | Maintain history                |
| Snowflaked     | Normalized dimension            |











> Grain : Grain defines the level of detail represented by each row in a fact table. Before designing a fact table, we must clearly define what one row represents.
Ex : Sales Fact table 

| Order_ID | Product | Customer | Sales |
| -------- | ------- | -------- | ----: |
| 1001     | Laptop  | John     |  1000 |

Grain : One row = One product in one order

> Why is Grain Important?
Defines the level of detail.
Prevents duplicate counting.
Helps identify the correct dimensions.
Determines the measures stored in the fact table.

Q: When do you define the grain?
Answer:
Grain should be defined before designing the fact table. It determines the fact table structure, measures, and required dimensions.

Q: Can a fact table have multiple grains?
No. A fact table should have one consistent grain. Mixing daily and monthly data in the same fact table can lead to incorrect aggregations and reporting.

> keys :
| Natural Key              | Surrogate Key            |
| ------------------------ | ------------------------ |
| Business-generated       | System-generated         |
| Has business meaning     | No business meaning      |
| May change               | Never changes            |
| Comes from source system | Created in the warehouse |
| Example: Employee_ID     | Example: Employee_Key    |

Q: Which key do you use in Fact Tables?
Surrogate Keys. Fact tables reference dimension tables using surrogate keys because they are stable and support historical tracking.


> Bridge Table
A Bridge Table is used to resolve a many-to-many relationship between a fact table and a dimension table.
Why do we need a Bridge Table?
Suppose one employee works on multiple projects.
One project also has multiple employees.
Relationship
Employee ←→ Project
This is Many-to-Many, which cannot be represented directly in a Star Schema.

Dim_Employee
      |
      |
 Bridge_Table
      |
      |
 Dim_Project

Q: Is Bridge Table a Fact Table?
No. It stores relationships. Not business measures.

| Bridge Table          | Fact Table               |
| --------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Resolves Many-to-Many | Stores business measures |
| Mostly Foreign Keys   | Measures + Foreign Keys  |
| No Sales Amount       | Sales Amount, Quantity   |

> Conformed Dimension
A Conformed Dimension is a dimension table that is shared by multiple fact tables. It has the same meaning, structure, and data across the data warehouse, ensuring consistent reporting.
Suppose you have three business processes:
Sales
Orders
Returns
Each process has its own fact table.
The Customer Dimension is shared by all three fact tables -> So it is a Conformed Dimension.
          Dim_Customer
               |
---------------------------------
|              |                |
Fact_Sales  Fact_Orders   Fact_Returns

> Why do we need Conformed Dimensions?
Consistent reporting
Reusable dimensions
Easier maintenance
Supports enterprise-wide analytics

> Role Playing Dimension 
Same dimension used multiple times within the same fact table.
ex:
Fact_Order
Order_Date
Ship_Date
Delivery_Date

All reference: Dim_Date

> Difference :

| Conformed Dimension                | Role-Playing Dimension                               |
| ---------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| Shared across multiple fact tables | Same dimension used multiple times in one fact table |
| Example: Customer                  | Example: Date                                        |

